The video and img tags are also called as the replaced elements because the content of these elements is replaced by an external source(image or video), which is
defined outside the scope of the regular document structure like in case of standard HTML elements like <p> or <div>, the browser knows that their content can be
text or some other tags which can be format easily using CSS but in case of replaced elements we cannot modify the internal content(image or video) of these elements.
We can only modify the outer appearance like margin, padding, border etc.
That's why the internal appearance of these elements is independent or out of scope of the CSS formating model.


An audio element is a 'conditional replaced element'. If the 'controls' attribute is defined while writing the audio tag, then it is going to act as a replaced element.
The browser adds UI controls like play, pause, seeking bar when we add 'controls' attribute which are generated by the browser engine. 
This is done independent of the CSS styling rules, and we cannot modify these UI buttons using CSS selectors on the audio tag. Hence the internal appearance of
audio element with 'control' attribute is independent or out of scope of the CSS formating model.

Without the controls attribute, we will not see the audio controls(play, pause, seeking bar) the audio element will not occupy any space on the screen, it will not 
have any dimensions. It can play audio if an autoplay attribute is written or via JavaScript.
So as there's no browser generated UI controls or content. The internal content of the element(the audio is playing) is not replaced, hence it acts as a non-replaced 
element.
